insert into public.posts
  (slug, title, dek, excerpt, category, content, author_name, author_bio, read_minutes, tags, is_published, published_at)
values (
  'the-geometry-of-habits',
  'The Geometry of Habits',
  'Why small changes compound, and how to design routines that actually stick.',
  'Why small changes compound, and how to design routines that actually stick.',
  'Essay',
  '<p class="animate-fade-in-up">We think habits are about willpower. They''re not. They''re about geometry.</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-1">A habit is a trajectory. Each repetition is a point on a curve. The curve''s shape — its slope, its inflection points, its asymptotes — determines whether the habit survives or collapses.</p>

        <h2>The Compound Curve</h2>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-2">James Clear popularized the 1% rule: get 1% better daily, and you''re 37x better in a year. The math is clean: 1.01^365 ≈ 37.8. But the geometry is messier.</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-3">Real progress isn''t a smooth exponential. It''s a series of plateaus punctuated by breakthroughs. The curve looks like stairs, not a ramp. Most people quit on the flat parts, not the steep ones.</p>

        <blockquote class="animate-fade-in-up delay-4">
          "We overestimate what we can do in a day, and underestimate what we can do in a decade."
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Three Geometric Principles</h2>

        <h3>1. Slope > Position</h3>
        <p class="animate-fade-in-up">Your current level matters less than your rate of change. A beginner with a steep slope overtakes an expert with a flat one. The question isn''t "How good am I?" — it''s "Am I improving?"</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-1">This is why starting small works. A habit with a sustainable slope beats an ambitious one that flatlines. Reading one page daily (slope: 365 pages/year) beats reading fifty pages once a month (slope: 600 pages/year, but zero for 29 days).</p>

        <h3>2. Friction Shapes the Curve</h3>
        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-2">Every habit has activation energy — the friction between intention and action. High friction = shallow slope. Low friction = steep slope.</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-3">Reduce friction geometrically:
        <ul class="animate-fade-in-up delay-4">
          <li><strong>Spatial:</strong> Put the book on your pillow. The guitar on its stand. The running shoes by the door.</li>
          <li><strong>Temporal:</strong> Anchor to existing routines. After coffee, write. After brushing, floss.</li>
          <li><strong>Cognitive:</strong> Decide once. "I write at 7am" removes the daily decision tax.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>

        <h3>3. Identity Is the Asymptote</h3>
        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-5">Habits approach an identity asymptote. You don''t "build a reading habit" — you become a reader. The habit is the proof; the identity is the limit.</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-6">This is why "I''m trying to quit smoking" fails but "I''m not a smoker" works. The former fights the old identity. The latter claims the new one. Each action votes for the identity. The curve bends toward the story you tell yourself.</p>

        <h2>Designing for Geometry</h2>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up">Don''t set goals. Set trajectories.</p>

        <ul class="animate-fade-in-up delay-1">
          <li><strong>Minimum viable action:</strong> What''s the smallest version that still counts? Do that.</li>
          <li><strong>Never miss twice:</strong> One miss is a point. Two misses define a new slope.</li>
          <li><strong>Measure the derivative:</strong> Track rate of change, not absolute position.</li>
          <li><strong>Design the environment:</strong> Make the right action the path of least resistance.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Long View</h2>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-2">A year from now, you''ll be somewhere on a curve. The curve''s shape is being decided today — by the friction you remove, the identity you claim, the small actions you repeat.</p>

        <p class="animate-fade-in-up delay-3">You don''t rise to the level of your goals. You fall to the level of your systems. And your systems are just geometry made visible.</p>',
  'Alex Chen',
  'Writer, reader, occasional builder of things.',
  6,
  ARRAY['Habits', 'Craft', 'Growth']::text[],
  true,
  '2026-09-03'::timestamptz
)
